<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Saint Maximin</h1><p class="st-meta">1139 CE – 1669 CE (approx.) · capital Trier</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the oldest and richest abbeys of the Rhineland, outside Trier, whose long fight to stay independent of the archbishops ended in 1669.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximin-of-trier">Saint Maximin's</a> Abbey grew around the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Maximin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> in the 4th century and became a Benedictine abbey. It was richly endowed by Frankish kings and became an imperial abbey. In 1139 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III</a> placed it under the Archbishop of Trier, beginning centuries of dispute over its status.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbey held extensive estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> region and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and was a centre of the 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorze-abbey">Gorze</a> reform movement. Its abbots claimed to be princes of the Empire with lands of their own, while the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> claimed the abbey as their territory, fighting the case in imperial courts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1669 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-council">Imperial Aulic Council</a> confirmed the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier's</a> lordship, ending the abbey's claim to independence. The abbey was dissolved under French rule in 1802; its rebuilt church survives in Trier as a school and event hall.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-saint-maximin.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Saint Maximin" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Maximin_(Stich).gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1139 CE – 1669 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Moselle Franconian German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>St. Maximin's Abbey, Trier</dd></dl></aside></div>
